    "caption": "Pepys buried gold six inches down. In 1667, Dutch ships raided the Medway, and Pepys moved his gold. He sent his wife and father away with a bag of gold. They buried it in the garden at Brampton. In October, he dug for it by night. The bags had rotted, and the coins lay only six inches down. He wrote that being short a hundred pieces made him mad. He and his clerk went back by night and sifted the soil. In the end, he decided his loss was not great. Local lore says coins may still lie in Brampton.",
